.TH "jetpipe" "8" "20261008" "ltsp" ""
.SH "NAME"
jetpipe \- share a printer device on a TCP port
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBjetpipe\fR [\fB\-d\fR] \fIdevice\fR \fIport\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBjetpipe\fR copies everything sent to TCP \fIport\fR to the printer
\fIdevice\fR, so that a print server can reach a printer attached to this
machine as a raw (JetDirect) network printer \(em in CUPS, a queue with the
device URI \fBsocket://\fIhost\fB:\fIport\fR.
.PP
One connection is served at a time; a printer cannot interleave two jobs.
The device is opened for each job and never created: a job sent while the
printer is unplugged is refused at once, and the print server retries. A job
that sends nothing for ten minutes is abandoned, so that a client that went
away without closing its connection cannot hold the printer.
.PP
On an LTSP client \fBjetpipe\fR is started by
\fBltsp\-printer@\fIn\fB.service\fR for every \fBPRINTER_\fIn\fB_DEVICE\fR in
lts.conf(5); there is normally no need to run it by hand.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-debug\fR
Stay in the foreground and log to stderr. Without it \fBjetpipe\fR detaches
and logs nothing.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show a short usage text.
.SH "EXAMPLE"
.PP
.nf
jetpipe \-d /dev/usb/lp0 9100
.fi
.PP
shares the first USB printer on port 9100.
.SH "NOTES"
.PP
Serial printers (\fI/dev/tty*\fR) are no longer supported and are refused.
.PP
There is no access control: anyone who can reach the port can print.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
lts.conf(5), cupsd(8)
